8. Master Dissertation Scope — GCRP 2 (VMAI)

Status: Proposed scope only. No work conducted. No committee formed. No pre-registration timestamped.

Working title: Runtime-Verified, Design-Diverse Multi-Agent Intelligence under Correlated Faults: An Assurance-Case Approach.

8.1 Motivation from external evidence

Homogeneous multi-agent pipelines exhibit a documented correlated-agent blind spot. In the PlanFlip evaluation, GPT-4o and Llama-3.3-70B showed attack-success rates near zero but stealth scores of 1.00 — the manipulations were not detected. Reasoning-augmented models (DeepSeek-R1) achieved step-shift scores of 0.00 across all attacks.

Implication. Heterogeneous model diversity is a security prerequisite, not a performance optimisation.

8.2 Specification as a research contribution

A recent PRISMA systematic review of 38 studies reports that natural-language-to-LTL translation achieves syntactic correctness above 90% but suffers from low semantic correctness. For PDDL, syntactic correctness exceeds 96%, while semantic correctness verified via plan-validation tools remains between 24.8% and 35.1% for the best-performing models.

Literature anchor. The specification should be written against AgentVerify's formalism: LTL safety properties verified via model checking over orchestration finite-state machines, treating the LLM as a non-deterministic oracle within the FSM. AgentVerify provides a compositional library of 23 LTL templates for agent safety, organised into four categories. Runtime-monitoring semantics for finite traces (LTLf) should follow the standard three-valued (\top/\bot/?) semantics characterized by Bauer, Leucker, and Schallhart.

Specification kill criterion. If the specification achieves syntactic validity but fails semantic validation against ground-truth execution traces, the runtime-monitor claim is unsupported and the dissertation pivots to specification methodology as its contribution.

8.3 Proposed falsifiable claim

Define the primary estimand as the relative reduction in correlated-failure probability:

R = 1 - \frac{p_1}{p_0}, \quad p_0 = P(\mathrm{CF} \mid A_0), \; p_1 = P(\mathrm{CF} \mid A_5).

Proposed claim (H₁): A heterogeneous multi-agent architecture A_5 combining (a) design-diverse LLM, symbolic-solver, and model-checker agents; (b) LTL/PCTL runtime monitors; (c) causal state estimation; and (d) weighted-credibility voting achieves

R \ge 0.30

at a non-inferior task-success rate

P(\text{success} \mid A_5) \ge 0.85 - \delta_{\mathrm{NI}}.

8.4 Statistical design — corrected

(i) Primary efficacy (superiority on relative reduction).

The confirmatory claim is not merely "p_0 \neq p_1" but "R \ge 0.30." The inferential procedure tests the threshold directly:

H_0: R \le 0.30 \quad \text{vs.} \quad H_1: R > 0.30.

The test uses a cluster-aware bootstrap with B = 10{,}000 resamples, respecting the experimental clustering (scenarios, prompts, seeds, attack families). BCa confidence intervals are computed on R. The lower bound of the one-sided 95\% CI must exceed 0.30.

Sample-size planning approximation (to be re-derived from pilot p_0):

n_{\text{per arm}}^{\text{sup}} = \frac{(z_{1-\alpha} + z_{1-\beta})^2 \cdot [p_0(1-p_0) + p_1(1-p_1)]}{(p_0 - p_1)^2} \cdot D,

with p_1 = (1 - R)p_0, D \ge 1 for design effect.

(ii) Co-primary non-inferiority (task success).

H_0: p_{\text{succ}}(A_5) - p_{\text{succ}}(A_0) \le -\delta_{\mathrm{NI}} \quad \text{vs.} \quad H_1: p_{\text{succ}}(A_5) - p_{\text{succ}}(A_0) > -\delta_{\mathrm{NI}}.

Estimand: risk difference. Baseline: 0.85 is a fixed minimum acceptable success rate, not a baseline-derived reference; this must be justified by domain acceptability. Margin: \delta_{\mathrm{NI}} must be prespecified based on domain acceptability, not sample-size convenience. The EMA guideline on non-inferiority margin selection provides the methodological anchor. Missing outcomes: failed runs are counted as failures under intention-to-treat; protocol deviations are reported separately.

(iii) Multiplicity. Both analyses are confirmatory; overall Type I error controlled at \alpha = 0.05 via gatekeeping: the efficacy superiority test must be significant before the NI test is interpreted.

8.5 Ten-arm ablation design — corrected

The v5.0 design listed 10 arms but described them as a "full 2⁴ fractional factorial." This is incorrect. A full factorial has 16 configurations. The 10-arm design includes the baseline, four single-component arms, the complete system, and four leave-one-out arms.

Correction. Call it a ten-arm ablation design. It can estimate some useful contrasts but cannot identify all interactions independently. For a constrained dissertation, a fractional factorial route is permissible but requires explicit specification:

· Fraction: 2^{4-1} (eight runs) or 2^{4-1}_{\text{IV}} (resolution IV).
· Defining relation: e.g., D = ABC for resolution IV.
· Alias structure: In a resolution IV design, main effects are aliased with three-factor interactions, and two-factor interactions are aliased with other two-factor interactions.
· Assumed negligible: three-factor and higher-order interactions.

