Appendix A: Statistical Design Derivations for GCRP 2 (VMAI)

A.1 Primary Efficacy Estimand and Inference

Define the correlated-failure event \mathrm{CF} and the relative reduction estimand

R = 1 - \frac{p_1}{p_0}, \quad p_0 = P(\mathrm{CF} \mid A_0), \quad p_1 = P(\mathrm{CF} \mid A_5).

The confirmatory null and alternative hypotheses are

H_0: R \le 0.30 \quad \text{versus} \quad H_1: R > 0.30.

Inference proceeds via a cluster-aware nonparametric bootstrap with B = 10{,}000 resamples. The resampling unit is the experimental cluster \omega (scenario–prompt–seed–attack-family tuple), not the individual episode, in order to respect within-cluster dependence. For each bootstrap replicate b, draw clusters with replacement, compute \hat{R}^{(b)}, and obtain the bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) interval [\hat{R}_{\mathrm{BCa},\,\alpha},\, \hat{R}_{\mathrm{BCa},\,1-\alpha}] with one-sided \alpha = 0.05. The confirmatory claim requires \hat{R}_{\mathrm{BCa},\,0.05} > 0.30.

The BCa interval adjusts for median bias and skewness of the bootstrap distribution via the acceleration constant a, computed by jackknife deletion of one cluster at a time:

a = \frac{\sum_{\omega} (\hat{R}_{(\cdot)} - \hat{R}_{(-\omega)})^3}{6 \left[ \sum_{\omega} (\hat{R}_{(\cdot)} - \hat{R}_{(-\omega)})^2 \right]^{3/2}},

where \hat{R}_{(-\omega)} is the estimate with cluster \omega omitted and \hat{R}_{(\cdot)} is the jackknife mean.

A.2 Co-Primary Non-Inferiority Hypothesis

The non-inferiority (NI) hypothesis is fully specified as

H_0^{\mathrm{NI}}: p_{\mathrm{succ}}(A_5) - p_{\mathrm{succ}}(A_0) \le -\delta_{\mathrm{NI}}

H_1^{\mathrm{NI}}: p_{\mathrm{succ}}(A_5) - p_{\mathrm{succ}}(A_0) > -\delta_{\mathrm{NI}}.

The estimand is the risk difference (RD). The margin \delta_{\mathrm{NI}} must be prespecified based on domain acceptability, following the EMA guideline on non-inferiority and equivalence comparisons, which requires that the margin be “considerably lower than the effect size of the least effective treatment” and justified independently of sample-size convenience. The baseline success rate 0.85 is a fixed minimum acceptable rate, not a baseline-derived reference; this must be justified by domain-specific acceptability criteria.

Missing outcomes are handled under intention-to-treat: failed runs are counted as failures. Protocol deviations are reported separately and do not enter the primary analysis.

A.3 Sample Size Approximation

For the superiority test, the per-arm sample size approximation is

n_{\text{per arm}}^{\text{sup}} = \frac{(z_{1-\alpha} + z_{1-\beta})^2 \cdot [p_0(1-p_0) + p_1(1-p_1)]}{(p_0 - p_1)^2} \cdot D,

with p_1 = (1 - R)p_0, z_{1-\alpha} = 1.645 for one-sided \alpha = 0.05, z_{1-\beta} = 0.842 for power 0.80, and D \ge 1 the design effect accounting for clustering. This approximation is a placeholder pending derivation of p_0 from the pilot ablation (Step 4 of the execution sequence). The design effect D is estimated from the pilot as

D = 1 + (m - 1)\rho,

where m is the average cluster size and \rho is the intra-cluster correlation of the correlated-failure indicator.

A.4 Multiplicity Control via Gatekeeping

Both the superiority and NI analyses are confirmatory. Overall Type I error is controlled at \alpha = 0.05 via a serial gatekeeping procedure: the superiority test must reject H_0 before the NI test is interpreted. If the superiority test fails to reject, the NI test is not performed and no confirmatory claim of non-inferiority is made. This preserves the familywise error rate without alpha adjustment.

