Appendix B: Calibration Metrics Specification for GCRP 1

B.1 Expected Calibration Error with M = 15 Equal-Width Bins

Let N be the number of predictions and B_m the set of predictions in bin m, m = 1,\dots,M, where M = 15 bins are constructed equal-width over [0,1]. The Expected Calibration Error is

\mathrm{ECE} = \sum_{m=1}^{M} \frac{|B_m|}{N} \left| \mathrm{acc}(B_m) - \mathrm{conf}(B_m) \right|,

where \mathrm{acc}(B_m) is the empirical accuracy in bin m and \mathrm{conf}(B_m) is the mean predicted confidence in bin m. ECE is a binned, non-proper metric: it does not incentivise honest reporting of uncertainty in the same way as a proper scoring rule. This limitation is explicitly acknowledged, and Brier score is therefore designated co-primary.

B.2 Brier Score as Co-Primary Proper Scoring Rule

For binary classification with predicted probability \hat{p}_i and true label y_i \in \{0,1\}, the Brier score is

\mathrm{BS} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} (\hat{p}_i - y_i)^2.

The Brier score is a proper scoring rule: it is minimised in expectation when the reported probabilities equal the true conditional probabilities. Unlike ECE, it cannot be gamed by bin-boundary manipulation, and it measures both discrimination and calibration simultaneously.

B.3 Primary and Co-Primary Criteria

The GCRP 1 confirmatory claim requires both:

1. A relative reduction in ECE of at least 25% versus baseline at matched coverage, with the lower bound of the one-sided 95% BCa bootstrap interval exceeding the 25% threshold.
2. No deterioration in Brier score relative to baseline (a non-inferiority criterion on the proper scoring rule).

This two-metric design prevents the pathology in which a system improves on a binned, non-proper metric while degrading on the underlying probabilistic quality of its predictions.

