Appendix C: Fractional Factorial Design for GCRP 2 Ablation

C.1 The Ten-Arm Ablation as a Screening Design

The ten-arm design (A_0 through A_9) is a screening design, not a full factorial. It includes the baseline (A_0), four single-component arms (A_1–A_4), the complete system (A_5), and four leave-one-out arms (A_6–A_9). This design can estimate main effects and the composite-system effect, but it cannot identify all two-factor interactions independently. The ablation kill criterion is stated in terms of the composite-system incremental benefit:

\Delta_{A_5} - \max(\Delta_{A_1}, \dots, \Delta_{A_4}) \ge 0.15,

where \Delta_{A_j} is the relative reduction in correlated-failure probability for arm j.

C.2 Fractional Factorial Alternative: 2^{4-1}_{\mathrm{IV}}

For a dissertation with sufficient resources, the four components (diversity, monitors, causal estimation, weighted voting) may be studied in a 2^{4-1} fractional factorial with resolution IV. The defining relation is

I = ABCD,

where A, B, C, D denote the four two-level factors. This design requires eight runs rather than sixteen, at the cost of aliasing structure.

C.3 Alias Structure

In a resolution IV design:

· Main effects are aliased with three-factor interactions.
· Two-factor interactions are aliased with other two-factor interactions.
· Three-factor and higher-order interactions are assumed negligible.

The alias structure for the defining relation I = ABCD is:

A = BCD, \quad B = ACD, \quad C = ABD, \quad D = ABC,

AB = CD, \quad AC = BD, \quad AD = BC.

This means, for example, that the observed contrast for A estimates \ell_A + \ell_{BCD}, and the observed contrast for AB estimates \ell_{AB} + \ell_{CD}. Main effects are clear of other main effects and of two-factor interactions, but two-factor interactions are not separable from one another without additional assumptions or follow-up runs.

C.4 Interaction Estimand vs. Composite Benefit

The 15-percentage-point criterion in §8.5 is a composite-system incremental benefit hypothesis, not a factorial interaction. A genuine interaction estimand for factors i and j is

\text{Interaction}_{ij} = \Delta_{A_{ij}} - (\Delta_{A_i} + \Delta_{A_j}) - \Delta_{A_0},

where \Delta_{A_{ij}} is the effect with both factors present, \Delta_{A_i} and \Delta_{A_j} are the single-factor effects, and \Delta_{A_0} is the baseline. The ten-arm design can estimate some, but not all, of these interaction contrasts; the fractional factorial provides a more systematic basis for interaction estimation under the negligible-higher-order assumption.

