Appendix F: Energy Accounting Three-Tier Framework for GCRP 3

F.1 Tier I: Thermodynamic Lower Bound

The Landauer bound states that erasing one bit of information at temperature T dissipates at least

E_{\mathrm{Landauer}} = k_B T \ln 2

of energy as heat. This is a thermodynamic lower bound, not a measured quantity. It applies only to logically irreversible operations; reversible computation discards no bits and is not subject to this bound.

F.2 Tier II: Measured Hardware Energy

Tier II reports on-package power measurement at the hardware package boundary. This is the empirical energy consumption of the physical system, including all overheads (memory access, interconnect, control). It is reported in Joules per operation or Joules per inference, with instrumentation specified.

F.3 Tier III: Model-Specific Computational Proxy

Tier III reports a model-specific computational proxy (e.g., FLOPs, MACs, or a weighted operation count) for cross-model comparison when direct hardware measurement is unavailable. Tier III is not a physical energy measure and must never be presented alongside Tier I or Tier II as if it were commensurable.

F.4 Joules per Bayesian Surprise

The proposed metric for GCRP 3 is Joules per unit Bayesian surprise, defined as

\mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{surprise}} = \frac{E_{\mathrm{measured}}}{D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{\mathrm{post}} \| p_{\mathrm{prior}})},

where D_{\mathrm{KL}} is the Kullback–Leibler divergence between posterior and prior. The computational boundary, energy instrumentation, and surprise estimation procedure are specified as [P] in §7. The proposed claim is that reversible linear inference with ReLU-avoidance reduces \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{surprise}} by \ge 15\% at fixed accuracy.

